==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|baby-sit}}

===Etymology===
{{back-form|en|babysitter}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈbeɪ.bi.sɪt/}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-babysit.ogg|a=US}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|babysit<,++,babysat:++[uncommon]>}}

# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To [[watch]] or [[tend]] someone else's child for a period of time, often for [[money]].
#: {{syn|en|childsit}}
#: {{ux|en|My daughter is '''babysitting''' for the Morgans at number ten, who are going out on a date night.}}
#: {{ux|en|We need someone to '''babysit''' our children while we go to the theater.}}
#* {{quote-journal
|1=en
|date=May 27, 2012
|author=Nathan Rabin
|title=TV: Review: THE SIMPSONS (CLASSIC): “New Kid On The Block” (season 4, episode 8; originally aired 11/12/1992)
|work=The Onion AV Club
|url=http://www.avclub.com/articles/new-kid-on-the-block,75341/
|page=
|passage=Bart eventually gets Laura to '''babysit''' while Homer and Marge eat at the Sea Captain’s all-you-can-eat seafood joint, The Frying Dutchman.
|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20120531031525/http://www.avclub.com/articles/new-kid-on-the-block,75341/
|archivedate=31 May 2012}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|informal}} To watch or tend a thing or process without normally intervening in it, e.g. as a precaution should an emergency happen.
#: {{ux|en|The reaction takes several hours, so we leave a graduate student to '''babysit''' it.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|informal}} To watch or attend anything or anyone more closely than ought to be needed; to have to help or coax too much.
#: {{syn|en|coddle|q1=partial}}
#: {{ux|en|He left me to '''babysit''' the new guy while he got some work done.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2016|author=Christopher Vasey|title=Nazi Intelligence Operations in Non-Occupied Territories|page=175
|passage=It was observed by the FBI personnel assigned to “'''babysit'''” agent Tricycle that his egregiously excessive spending was causing unwanted attention {{...}}}}

